## Why GPU isn't 27× faster end-to-end

W44-RECON-DEEP/A7 measured the GPU butteraugli kernel itself at 27.6×
faster than CPU on 1024² multi-resolution. End-to-end encode speedup is
much smaller because:

1. **Buttloop is only ~20-30% of encode wall** at e8/e9 (per the W44-170
   wall-time breakdown).
2. **Butteraugli compare is only ~30-50% of buttloop wall** (the rest is
   transform+quantize+reconstruct+gab+EPF+per-tile-distance, all on CPU).
3. **Host-side sRGB encode** (linear-f32 → sRGB-u8 packed) for the GPU
   pipeline is non-trivial — the W44-phase3-B1 implementation uses an
   8193-entry LUT that drops the per-pixel cost to ~5 ns vs ~100 ns for
   the scalar `powf` path. Without the LUT the host-side pack alone
   wiped out the GPU win (an early test showed GPU 0.86× CPU; with the
   LUT we're now 0.97-1.07× CPU).

The 0-7% speedups we see are consistent with these constraints. To get
closer to A7's predicted 8-12% encode-level win, the next chunk should:
1. Skip the sRGB round-trip entirely by using the
   `butteraugli_gpu::Butteraugli::compute_with_reference_from_linear_planes`
   internals API (passes pre-uploaded GPU f32 linear-RGB handles
   directly, no host-side conversion). This requires also routing the
   reference side through the same API which currently only accepts
   sRGB-u8 — needs an upstream butteraugli-gpu change.
2. Allocate persistent GPU handles for the recon planes and reuse them
   across iters (currently each iter re-uploads via the sRGB-u8 path,
   which itself is fine because butteraugli-gpu's `pack_srgb_into_packed_u32_handle`
   already takes that input — the cost is in the host-side conversion,
   not the GPU upload).

These are deferred to a W44-phase3-B1 follow-on chunk; the current
landing is a foundation that proves the trait + dispatch + fallback
mechanism is correct and CPU-bit-identical at default.

## Reproduction

```
CUDA_PATH=/usr/local/cuda CARGO_TARGET_DIR=$HOME/work/zen/jxl-encoder-shared-target \
  cargo run --release \
  --features 'gpu-butteraugli butteraugli-loop ssim2-loop parallel __expert' \
  --example w44_phase3_b1_gpu_buttloop_ab -- \
  --output benchmarks/w44_phase3_b1_gpu_buttloop_ab_2026-05-23.tsv
```

Set `JXL_W44_PHASE3_B1_DEBUG=1` to see the per-encode backend selection
(CPU vs GPU, including fallback path triggers).

## DO NOT

- DO NOT enable `gpu-butteraugli` by default in production builds. The
  feature is opt-in at the cargo level AND at the API level
  (`with_gpu_butteraugli`); both layers must be flipped explicitly.
- DO NOT cite "FMA precision" for the 0.02-0.05% bytes/SSIM2 drift (per
  W44-66 user correction). The drift comes from the GPU's sRGB-u8
  round-trip, NOT FMA precision.
- DO NOT widen the cell set to images < 256² without first measuring —
  the GPU has ~3 ms fixed kernel-launch overhead that swamps the win
  on very small inputs.
- DO NOT spawn a follow-on attempting to enable the GPU backend
  by default until either (a) the sRGB round-trip is eliminated via
  the `internals` API or (b) the speedup measurement shows ≥ 5% encode
  wall on real corpus cells. Today's measurement (0-7% with one
  regression cell) is not strong enough to justify default-flip.
